---
import { url } from '../utils/urls';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const criteria=[
 {id:'in-use',short:'Works in use',title:'Does it work in use—or only in a screenshot?',body:['Attractive output can still be wrong. In Overlords & Outlaws, a flat card-lane redesign made the screen tidier but turned a physical card game into a dashboard. I rejected it and restored the 3D board, with cards that feel like printed objects.','The same question changed the game itself. My first prototype worked, but played too much like existing combat card games. I kept the distinctive idea—winning through secure succession—and rebuilt around it.'],link:'/projects/overlords-and-outlaws/',label:'See the game and the decisions'},
 {id:'specific',short:'Specific, not generic',title:'Is it specific enough to belong here?',body:['A generic king in a generic costume can be beautifully rendered and still fail the brief. Each of the 84 portraits starts from a specific century, court, wardrobe, setting, and source record. The prompts set material, palette, and composition; the review asks whether the result belongs to this person and this game.','That habit predates generative AI. For Alison Wonderland’s “Summersalts” in Tetris Beat, I worked from the artist’s symbols and colors to evoke the feeling of a live performance. The visual idea had a reason to be that particular idea.'],link:'/work/#summersalts',label:'See the production work'},
 {id:'uncertainty',short:'Honest uncertainty',title:'Is it honest about what it does not know?',body:['A confident presentation can conceal uncertain information. The portrait briefs distinguish evidence from invention. A later audit calls out speculative armor and anachronistic backgrounds instead of treating a convincing image as proof.','ColorBreak makes the same decision in an interface. Low-confidence OCR lines remain visible. Missing prices never become zero. Uncertainty appears beside the answer, where it can affect the person’s decision.'],link:'/projects/colorbreak/',label:'Explore uncertainty in the interface'},
 {id:'context',short:'The real context',title:'Does it hold up where it will live?',body:['A portrait can look wonderful full-screen and fail on a small card. The composition brief positions the head, eyes, shoulders, and props inside the printed frame opening. I review contact sheets and the rendered game because that is where the work has to succeed.','On Tetris Beat’s “Falling Fantasy,” the illusion of a twisting tunnel comes from a straight cylinder and one unlit shader. It had to work from an iPhone 6s to 4K Mac screens. Constraints are part of the visual design.'],link:'/work/#falling-fantasy',label:'See the shader-driven work'},
 {id:'critique',short:'Actionable critique',title:'Could someone act on this critique?',body:['“This feels off” leaves the next person guessing. My art-direction rubric asks for a verdict, an exact screenshot, the blocking problem, its cost, and a concrete fix. It ranks the next improvements and protects what is already working.','I have given daily critique to production teams and taught design to working professionals. The standard is the same: say what works, identify what does not, and explain the difference precisely enough to make the next pass better.'],link:'/projects/ai-art-direction-review/',label:'Read the critique standard'},
 {id:'challenge',short:'Try to break it',title:'Has someone tried to break the idea?',body:['Before ColorBreak’s second version, I organized three rounds of adversarial review by defined AI expert personas. Each was briefed to falsify the specification and classify findings by severity. These were AI reviewers, not a human research panel.','I adjudicated the results: accept changes that prevent false authority or make the core job executable; defer claims that require data we do not have; dismiss unsupported recommendations. More feedback is useful only when someone owns the decision.'],link:'/projects/colorbreak/',label:'See the review and adjudication'},
 {id:'evidence',short:'Earn the claim',title:'Does the evidence earn the claim?',body:['A good-looking number is not a result. The final 200-game decision audit for Overlords & Outlaws found a lower share of plausible tradeoffs than an earlier version: 11.6% versus 24.6%. I keep that limitation visible. Automated continuations do not prove the game is interesting to humans.','For Phase-RS, I keep separate experiments separate. The initial nondeterminism finding and the later 100-pair result have different setups. A clear explanation of what was measured is more useful than a dramatic chart that blurs the difference.'],link:'/projects/phase-rs-ai-measurement/',label:'Inspect the measurement evidence'},
 {id:'provenance',short:'Credit & provenance',title:'Can you show where it came from?',body:['Every installed Overlords & Outlaws portrait has an exact prompt and an original output record. Revisions remain inspectable. The generated work is labeled as generated, and historical reconstruction is not presented as a surviving likeness.','I apply the same discipline to collaboration. My ArtStation pages separate my modeling, rigging, animation, and direction from other artists’ textures, concepts, and production work. At Amber, I directed models trained on wholly owned, rights-cleared assets and developed an ethical AI framework used across the studios.'],link:'/projects/overlords-and-outlaws/',label:'Inspect the art provenance'}
];
---
<Base title="How I judge AI output — Bradd McBrearty" description="Eight questions I use to judge AI-generated work: function, specificity, uncertainty, context, actionable critique, adversarial review, evidence, and provenance.">
<div class="wrap"><header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">A working philosophy / Eight questions</p><h1>Options are cheap.<br/><em>Judgment is the work.</em></h1><p class="lead">These are the questions I ask of an image, a layout, a card face, a game rule, or a pull request. Each has an example from my own practice.</p></header><div class="essay-layout"><aside class="essay-toc" aria-label="Essay contents">{criteria.map((c,i)=><a href={`#${c.id}`}>0{i+1} / {c.short}</a>)}</aside><div>{criteria.map((c,i)=><section class="essay-criterion" id={c.id}><p class="eyebrow">Question 0{i+1}</p><h2>{c.title}</h2>{c.body.map(b=><p>{b}</p>)}<a class="text-link" href={url(c.link)}>{c.label} ↗</a></section>)}<p class="lead">The tools change every few months. These questions don’t. AI makes the work faster; it doesn’t get a lower bar.</p></div></div></div>
</Base>
